NUM-16: keep the notch flow of a partly open V-notch weir

A V-notch weir with a setting below 1 is computed as a trapezoidal weir
whose crest is raised by (1 - setting) * height. The triangular ends of that
trapezoid are the notch itself, but their flow used Cd2, the end coefficient
of TRAPEZOIDAL weirs, which defaults to 0 and is not documented for V-notch
weirs. A setting of 0.999 therefore removed almost all of the weir's flow.
Use the V-notch coefficient (Cd, or its Cd curve value) for the ends of a
partly open V-notch weir; the rectangular centre strip is unchanged. Fully
open V-notch weirs and all other weir types are unchanged.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply NUM-16_swmm530.patch

diff --git a/src/legacy/engine/link.c b/src/legacy/engine/link.c
index 31925e1..9e363f7 100644
--- a/src/legacy/engine/link.c
+++ b/src/legacy/engine/link.c
@@ -2435,7 +2435,10 @@ void weir_getFlow(int j, int k,  double head, double dir, int hasFlapGate,
         y = (1.0 - Link[j].setting) * Link[j].xsect.yFull;
         length = xsect_getWofY(&Link[j].xsect, y) * UCF(LENGTH);
         *q1 = cDisch1 * length * pow(h, 1.5);
-        *q2 = Weir[k].cDisch2 * Weir[k].slope * pow(h, 2.5);
+
+        // --- a partly open V-notch keeps its own coeff. for the notch
+        *q2 = (Weir[k].type == VNOTCH_WEIR ? cDisch1 : Weir[k].cDisch2) *
+              Weir[k].slope * pow(h, 2.5);
     }
 
     // --- convert CMS flows to CFS
